  "query_preview": "..\nKey Message 1...Conditions are favorable for isolated to scattered \nsevere thunderstorms through this evening. \n\nGOES water vapor imagery shows multiple shortwave troughs embedded \nin a deep trough across the Great Lakes region this morning. One \nshortwave in particular will move across the eastern Great Lakes \nregion today. Deep convection is ongoing near a warm front across \neastern Ontario this morning. Showers and a few thunderstorms are \nexpected to move into the Niagara Frontier this morning.\n\nMid level height falls, DPVA, and upper level divergence ahead of \nthe trough are expected to contribute to a background of large scale \nascent today. A warm front will move across the region today \nushering in deep southwesterly flow and moisture with dewpoints \nclimbing into the upper 60s to low 70s today. The warm front will be \nthe first focus of convection today with showers and thunderstorms \nmoving east across western NY. This convection will move into north-\ncentral NY this afternoon, while additional convection north of Lake \nOntario moves into the North Country as well, and may lead to a more \nprolonged period of rain east of Lake Ontario today. Instability is \nexpected build slowly across the region due to debris clouds and \nshowers. Some hi-res models such as the HRRR shows a lull of \nconvection across western NY this afternoon. An increasing southwest \nflow will also contribute a stable lake shadow east of Lake Erie \nthis afternoon. Additional showers and thunderstorms are expected to \nform along lake breeze boundaries across the Niagara Frontier and \neast of Lake Erie this afternoon. \n\nA strong cold front will move through the region this evening and \nanother round of showers and a few thunderstorms are expected. The \nsevere risk will continue this evening with strong low level \nconvergence and deep moisture ahead of the front. \n\nWhile there remains uncertainty in overall coverage of \nthunderstorms today, the combination of large scale forcing, \nforecast surface based instability over 1000 J/Kg and curved \nhodographs supports isolated to scattered severe thunderstorms \nthrough this evening. The SPC maintains a Slight Risk across the \nentire area with damaging winds as the main severe threat. An \nisolated tornado and severe hail can't be ruled out. The moist air \nmass will also lead to the risk of localized flooding across the \nregion. The greatest risk of flooding will likely be associated with \nboundaries where storms can backbuild.  \n\nCoverage of showers and especially thunder will diminish by mid to \nlate evening with the loss of diurnal instability. \n\nKey Message 2...Cool Wednesday, with a chance of a few lingering \nshowers and isolated thunderstorms.\n\nThe digging trough will briefly develop into a mid level closed low \nover western Quebec Wednesday. Deep moisture will be east of the \narea by Wednesday morning, but forcing for ascent from several \nshortwaves rounding the base of the trough coupled with cool air \naloft will still support a few scattered showers through the day, \nand possibly an isolated thunderstorm or two. Both intensity and \ncoverage will be far less than Tuesday. \n\nTemperatures will be quite cool Wednesday, close to 10F below \naverage for mid summer. Highs will struggle to reach the lower 70s \nfor lower elevations, with mid 60s across higher terrain. Lows \nWednesday night will drop back into the 50s in most areas, with mid \n40s for the Southern Tier valleys and Lewis County.",
